WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.191
Lab Studio is a static chemistry workspace for authoring and running schema-driven labs.

00:00:07.191 --> 00:00:14.311
Teachers build structured process maps, and students practice the same workflows in the simulator.

00:00:14.311 --> 00:00:20.389
Hash routes make each lab and technique easy to share from static hosting.

00:00:20.389 --> 00:00:25.719
This walkthrough covers the student flow first, then the teacher authoring flow.

00:00:25.719 --> 00:00:34.815
In Student Player, the learner sees the goal, mode toggle, equipment, workbench, process progress, feedback, and evidence panels together.

00:00:34.815 --> 00:00:43.910
Guided mode keeps the expected step visible. Assessment mode lets the same runtime run with less scaffolding.

00:00:43.910 --> 00:00:55.044
The shelf and workbench keep equipment state visible while each process node advances.

00:00:55.044 --> 00:01:07.105
Direct bench manipulation sends a typed action request through the simulator runtime.

00:01:07.105 --> 00:01:20.252
Feedback explains what changed, and the process map moves to the next required action.

00:01:20.252 --> 00:01:35.412
Evidence controls appear when measurements, calculations, or notebook entries are ready.

00:01:35.412 --> 00:01:47.522
Assessment mode uses the same lab definition, so teachers are not maintaining a separate activity.

00:01:47.522 --> 00:02:00.530
Reset returns the lab to its initial equipment and process state for another attempt.

00:02:00.530 --> 00:02:15.831
The hard-water demo is reference content built from general schema-backed actions.

00:02:15.831 --> 00:02:26.552
Standalone techniques isolate one skill, such as filtering and rinsing a precipitate.

00:02:26.552 --> 00:02:41.608
Learners can place equipment with pointer or drag interactions while the runtime records the same state transition.

00:02:41.608 --> 00:02:53.697
The keyboard path exposes source and target selection for the same underlying action.

00:02:53.697 --> 00:03:08.713
Confirm accessible action completes the expected step without requiring drag and drop.

00:03:08.713 --> 00:03:19.060
Technique practice can be used alone or embedded inside a larger lab.

00:03:19.060 --> 00:03:31.128
Lab Design Studio authors constrained LabDefinition JSON instead of arbitrary teacher code.

00:03:31.128 --> 00:03:45.244
Templates add supported actions and nodes that the simulator already understands.

00:03:45.244 --> 00:04:00.426
Adding a filtration template extends the process map and keeps the draft schema-driven.

00:04:00.426 --> 00:04:15.514
Details edits structured fields such as title, description, hints, action parameters, and feedback.

00:04:15.514 --> 00:04:29.641
Validation rules, retry paths, required equipment, and start node choices stay explicit in the authoring surface.

00:04:29.641 --> 00:04:44.965
Live preview runs the current draft through the same Student Player used by learners.

00:04:44.965 --> 00:04:56.491
Save draft stores local authoring work for this browser.

00:04:56.491 --> 00:05:06.825
Export and import round-trip the lab as portable JSON for review or publishing.

00:05:06.825 --> 00:05:19.918
Published bundled labs come from public JSON files and load through the same route path students use.

00:05:19.918 --> 00:05:33.047
Bundled techniques follow the same contract, so fixtures are not the publishing source of truth.

00:05:33.047 --> 00:05:47.112
Share the lab catalog with #/labs, link directly to #/play/hard-water-demo, or publish new JSON-backed content after validation and build checks pass.
